{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said: I have surely seen, etc.</b> One must examine why He repeated to say two seeings. Second, why was it necessary to specify and say ״אֲשֶׁר בְּמִצְרָיִם״ — and if Moses would not recognize the people of Elohim, it is not identified for him by this sign ״אֲשֶׁר בְּמִצְרָיִם,״ for there were many nations in Egypt. And perhaps it is because there was no nation oppressed and subjugated in Egypt like Israel — but the mind does not accept this, for it cannot be that Moses did not know that Israel are the people of HaShem and His inheritance." "Rather, HaShem intends to inform that He saw two seeings. The first is that the end had not yet arrived, and nevertheless <b>I have seen the affliction of My people</b>, and the affliction is <b>that is in Egypt</b> — meaning, along the lines of what they said there in tractate Shabbat (10a), that on account of two selas of weight, etc., the matter rolled about and our forefathers descended to Egypt. And the Tosafot raised the difficulty: but was not the exile already decreed at the Covenant Between the Parts? And they answered that it could have been in another place where Israel would not have been enslaved with such great pain and affliction. And this is what He says <b>that is in Egypt</b> — meaning, and for this aspect of the heaviness of the subjugation I see that although the end of the redemption has not yet arrived, the end regarding the particular aspect of the affliction with which they oppress and enslave them has arrived." "<b>Further,</b> He intends to say that He saw the suffering that exists in it, and further <b>I have seen the affliction of My people</b> — that they are the portion of HaShem, His people, and the affliction is that they are in Egypt, for if Israel tarry longer they are lost, Heaven forbid, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (in the Passover Haggadah): ״And had the Holy One, blessed be He, not brought our forefathers out of Egypt, then…״ — for if they had remained there longer they would have entered the fiftieth gate of impurity and there would have been no recovery; therefore He redeemed them immediately and did not delay even the time it would take for their dough to leaven. Although labor had already ceased from them and they were princes of Zoan, the place itself causes affliction, and this is the reason for His saying (Exodus 13:39) ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ לְהִתְמַהְמֵהַּ,״ and this is what the sage, blessed be He, alluded to in <b>that is in Egypt.</b>" "<b>Further,</b> He intends by saying <b>I have surely seen</b> two seeings: one, that the sifting of the holy sparks was completed and they no longer have any benefit in exile, as they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 9b; Pesahim 119b) on the verse ״וַיְנַצְּלוּ אֶת מִצְרָיִם״ — they made it like a מְצוּדָה (net/trap) that has no grain, and like a מְצוּלָה (sea-depth) that has no fish; and further, I have seen that their remaining there was a trap for them to bury them in the klipah of impurity, as I have written." "<b>And I have heard their cry.</b> Meaning, and further, their cry from trouble that has come upon Me, for they continued to do evil until they cried out a cry of pain of the heart, as I explained in the verse ״וַתַּעַל שַׁוְעָתָם וְגוֹ׳ מִן הָעֲבוֹדָה.״ And this is an introduction to the reason for His mission before the appointed time, which is to save them from trouble from now, that they would not be enslaved in that year when the Egyptians were being struck with great plagues — for from the day the plagues began in Egypt, the subjugation was removed from Israel, as I shall explain nearby in the verse ״וָאֵרֵד לְהַצִּילוֹ.״ Also, according to what I explained in His saying <b>I have surely seen</b> — that He intended regarding the trouble of Egypt, that the time had come to remove it — even so, an arousal is needed from those who are to be redeemed; therefore He said <b>And I have heard their cry.</b>" "And the verse's saying <b>from before his taskmasters</b> — after it mentioned the hearing of the outcry, it should rather have said only ״וְאֶת צַעֲקָתָם מִפְּנֵי נוֹגְשָׂיו שָׁמַעְתִּי״ (״And their outcry from before his taskmasters I have heard״). Further, it said <b>צַעֲקָתָם</b> in plural and <b>נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> in singular, and did not balance the grammatical form. It intends to say that the children of Israel — their hearts cried out to HaShem in prayer, and this supports what I explained regarding the verse ״וַתַּעַל שַׁוְעָתָם״ וְגוֹ׳ (Exodus 2:23). For had it said ״וְאֶת צַעֲקָתָם מִפְּנֵי נוֹגְשָׂיו שָׁמַעְתִּי״, it would have been understood that the outcry was because of the labor, but that is not so — rather, their outcry was in prayer before HaShem." "And the reason it said <b>צַעֲקָתָם</b> in plural and <b>נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> in singular is that the many who cry out are not equal in their outcry to HaShem; therefore it described them with a plural term. But in the measure of the taskmaster, there was one taskmaster over all of them together equally; therefore it said מִפְּנֵי <b>נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> in singular. And the reason for saying <b>מִפְּנֵי נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> is to allude that the reason He accepted the outcry of their hearts to HaShem is because they cried out from distress, as I explained in connection with the verse ״מִן הָעֲבוֹדָה״. Further, it may intend by saying <b>מִפְּנֵי נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> to allude here that the Egyptians' measure was filled to overflowing — to requite them for the evil they did — even though the time of redemption for Israel had not yet arrived. And this is another reason for the sending of Moses before the time of redemption." "<b>Further</b>, <b>מִפְּנֵי נוֹגְשָׂיו</b> alludes, in the manner of the verse (Isaiah 65:24): ״טֶרֶם יִקְרָאוּ וַאֲנִי אֶעֱנֶה״ — and here He informed that before the taskmaster's oppression, the Master alreadyperceived the outcry of His beloved children. And according to this approach, the reason for the singular form is to say: even the first oppression among them. And He stated the reason <b>כִּי יָדַעְתִּי אֶת מַכְאוֹבָיו</b> to say: even though they had not yet cried out, I know and pay attention to the pain of My people and My inheritance. In this too He revealed the intensity of His love for Israel, like a compassionate father, etc." "And according to the earlier approaches I explained, the intent of saying <b>כִּי יָדַעְתִּי</b> וְגוֹ׳ is to magnify the abundance of their troubles — that they could no longer endure them, for their pains multiplied in the revealed and in the hidden." "Further, it may intend to inform him <b>כִּי יָדַעְתִּי מַכְאוֹבָיו</b> — meaning, not that He received them from those who cry out, but rather He recognized and knew the measure of the pains. And this matter obligates, according to His attributes, to bring salvation, as I explained regarding the verse ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים״ — for by His seeing the distress of His creatures, His mercies multiply beyond the line of strict justice. And likewise, the matter of the intensity of knowing the pain of His beloved ones causes His mercies to multiply. And from the gathering together of all these reasons He said <b>וָאֵרֵד לְהַצִּילוֹ.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:21.136413334Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}